          "lem-qft-second-quantized-spectrum": "mathematical-premise",
          "lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-dirac-projections": "mathematical-premise",
          "def-qft-fock-and-test-spaces": "mathematical-premise"
        },
        "physical_scope": "State and observable definitions for the selected flat, noninteracting scalar and Dirac free-field models. Fields are test-smeared; local Dirac observables are even CAR combinations. Measurements use only constructed instruments. The planned charged-number domain remains an explicit conditional input.",
        "empirical_premises": [],
        "statement": "Use the fields, Fock spaces, vacua, coordinate conventions, and natural/SI\nnormalizations adopted in [[post-qft-free-field-model]]. For an open spacetime\nregion $O\\subseteq\\mathbb R^4$, a scalar test is a real\n$f\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb R)$ and a Dirac test is a\n$F\\in C_c^\\infty(O;\\mathbb C^4)$. These are compactly supported smooth test\nfunctions on the fixed inertial Minkowski chart.\n\nFor scalar tests use the bounded Weyl operators\n$$W_\\mu(f)=W_{\\rm Segal}(\\sqrt2 K_\\mu f),$$\nwhere $K_\\mu$ is the future-shell restriction and\n$W_{\\rm Segal}$ is the unitary generated by the closed Segal field, with the\nnormalization and phase convention of\n[[lem-qft-smeared-free-scalar]]. Define the scalar bounded observable algebra\n$\\mathfrak A_s(O)$ to be the norm closure of the unital $*$-algebra generated\nby these $W_\\mu(f)$. The real smeared field is symmetric on\n$\\mathcal D_{\\rm fin}$ and its self-adjoint closure has the exact multiplier\ndomain supplied by [[lem-qft-free-domain-observables]] and\n[[lem-qft-segal-weyl-domain]].\n\nFor Dirac tests, $\\Psi(F)$ is the bounded smeared CAR field of\n[[lem-qft-free-dirac-car-locality]]. Define $\\mathfrak A_D(O)$ as the norm\nclosure of the unital algebra generated by products containing an even number\nof these field operators and their adjoints, with all test supports in $O$.\n“Even” here means even field parity, or equivalently an even number of odd\nCAR field generators in each product. The even algebra is the selected local\nDirac observable algebra; an odd charged field is not included as a commuting\nlocal observable.\n\nThe selected vacuum states on bounded operators are the vector states\n$\\omega_s(A)=\\langle\\Omega_s,A\\Omega_s\\rangle$ and\n$\\omega_D(B)=\\langle\\Omega_D,B\\Omega_D\\rangle$. For the scalar Fock space,\ndefine the total number, Hamiltonian, and translation momentum components by\n$$N_s=d\\Gamma_s(1),\\qquad H_s=\\hbar c\\,d\\Gamma_s(\\omega_\\mu),\\qquad P_{s,j}=\\hbar\\,d\\Gamma_s(-k_j).$$\nHere $d\\Gamma_s$ acts on the $n$-particle sector by summing the displayed\none-particle multiplier over its $n$ arguments. Their maximal domains are\nthe square-integrability domains of those sector sums; in particular,\n$$D(N_s)=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge1}n^2\\|\\Psi_n\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThe sign in $P_{s,j}$ matches the field translation phase\n$\\omega_\\mu x^0-\\mathbf k\\cdot\\mathbf x$. For the Dirac Fock space, let $H$\nand $P_j$ be the mathematical translation generators of\n[[lem-qft-dirac-fermionic-translation-spectrum]]. Their one-particle\nmultipliers are respectively $(\\omega_\\mu,\\mathbf k)$ for particles and\n$(\\omega_\\mu,-\\mathbf k)$ for antiparticles, and the sector multiplier is\ntheir sum. Define the physical Dirac Hamiltonian and momentum by\n$H_D=\\hbar cH$ and $P_{D,j}=\\hbar P_j$, using the positive SI constants from\n[[def-qft-framework-units]]. Their exact domains are unchanged by these\nnonzero scale factors; their multipliers have units joules and momentum,\nrespectively.\n\nFor any of these self-adjoint operators $A$, a normalized state\n$\\Psi\\in D(A)$ has model mean $\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle$ and variance\n$\\operatorname{Var}_\\Psi(A)=\\|(A-\\langle\\Psi,A\\Psi\\rangle)\\Psi\\|^2$.\nThis requires the second spectral moment but does not require\n$\\Psi\\in D(A^2)$. Products of unbounded operators use their composition\ndomains.\n\nIf a signed charge parameter $q_{\\rm SI}\\in\\mathbb R$ measured in coulombs is supplied, the intended\nDirac charge-number operator is $Q_{\\rm SI}=q_{\\rm SI}(N_p-N_{\\bar p})$.\nHere $\\Pi_p$ and $\\Pi_{\\bar p}$ are the orthogonal projections onto\n$\\mathcal H_+$ and $\\overline{\\mathcal H_-}$; on the $n$-particle sector,\n$N_p$ and $N_{\\bar p}$ are the sums of the corresponding projections over\nthe $n$ tensor factors. Its candidate maximal direct-sum domain is\n$$D(Q_{\\rm SI})=\\left\\{\\Psi=(\\Psi_n):\\sum_{n\\ge0}\\left\\|q_{\\rm SI}\\sum_{r=1}^n(\\Pi_p-\\Pi_{\\bar p})^{(r)}\\Psi_n\\right\\|^2<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nThis is a candidate definition only; no self-adjointness or proved-domain\nclaim is asserted here.\n\nMeasurements in this model are assigned only when a state and a normalized\nfinite bounded Kraus instrument have actually been constructed. For such an\ninstrument, the adopted outcome probabilities and positive-probability\nconditional states are exactly those of\n[[post-qft-constructed-model-and-measurement]]. They are model probabilities,\nnot observed outcomes. The field definitions are test-smeared: neither\n$\\Phi_\\mu(x)$ nor $\\Psi(x)$ is a pointwise operator supplied here, and the\nsmearing results do not define coincident products or a diagonal restriction.",
        "provenance": {
          "statement": "literature-derived",
          "proof": "not-applicable"
        },
        "sources": {
          "references": [
